#edb/ref 17592186549656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549848 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "75ef87bd-bbc6-5f5c-a6b4-1dd96adfb505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549889 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549889 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549889 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549889 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549889 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549889 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549889 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549889 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186549785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549889 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b6a3ec9d-c696-5081-a8d8-ff61a574a27d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549922 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186549671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549922 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549922 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549922 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186549671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549922 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fbc8d1ae-cf76-5d8c-94ef-a0c5ef612072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549932 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549932 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549932 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549932 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8fb46d101101d3d2bdb4afcfcc1436f1a62137389db2ffc3fde0cff71054e7f1.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=27-0.5x.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $y$ when $x=16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1131d502-6491-586e-9e5c-4aa6c2f2c8de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549932 #edb/ref 1074 "We can estimate the required value by substituting $x=16$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =27-0.5x \\\\  & =27-0.5(16) \\\\  & =27-8 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=16,$ we have $y≈19.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=16$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549941 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549941 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549941 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549941 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/df/dff61f08e09f21935f616788e54dfb56e0d0a89f38cce9fbc6dc7333d21ba5f1.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nUse the trend line to predict the value of $x$ when $y=80.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549941 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b530d38-bb33-510e-b9cb-65e2c39387f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549941 #edb/ref 1074 "To make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $y=80$ on the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/e5/e5923b6c9f0ac4dcf2aee35e67877c425b44f81830bb9f0865c5e21acf81e3f4.png)\n\n- Then, we move right until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/61/613849314df8014335f7493f9b6b64cb4f2ecd1e585593416f416f1854e12ffa.png)\n\n- Finally, we move down until we intersect the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/45/4523e742266e3f2d2e759c6e65eb2a7f2bd31e8f4b8473b8e6bfbabec067c9d9.png)\n\nTherefore, when $y=80,$ we obtain $x≈15.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549958 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b5/b5d153746d837a857918028aceb623a5c2b9267f00b6baaceb2f24a8172587ee.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nUse the trend line to predict the value of $y$ when $x=70.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44c1c5d8-2db1-5cbc-a653-89f6c7b7e591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549958 #edb/ref 1074 "To make our prediction, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we start at $x=70$ on the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/ee/ee670899ebf962939a77959c58219502907681d8cf84329f402b67f6f09a24ba.png)\n\n* Then, we move up until we intersect the trend line.\n\n![](images/f6/f6d34d0c6b95b4b3d5ecf720346ca1e0008ac86c42e8024a2435e2bf792e5031.png)\n\n- Finally, we move left until we intersect the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/02/0217edcb171859f1aec207d5c248c483a3147dbe74aed277f1313009dbc317c9.png)\n\nTherefore, when $x=70,$ we obtain $y≈5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549970 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6f/6f38e64442bb383f218dede5a88740653f958864d3284ce09c3e1bb99ed7e421.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the time (in hours) some college students spend studying the night before an exam and the time they spend sleeping that night. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the time a student spends studying if that student gets only $3.75$ hours of sleep.\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(2,7.5)$ and $(4,5).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73f7b139-c8da-5c53-9a35-10e77ca9a819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549970 #edb/ref 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/9e/9e1653b2acbe5c5924e2d562308b2640c64f317ed7500fba4e149c926c9205ab.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,7.5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,5).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-7.5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2.5)}{2} \\\\  & =-1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1.25$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-1.25x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.25x+b \\\\ 5 & =-1.25(4)+b \\\\ 5 & =-5+b \\\\ b & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-1.25x+10.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=3.75$ into the equation above and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1.25x+10 \\\\ 3.75 & =-1.25x+10 \\\\ 1.25x & =6.25 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the student spends approximately $5$ hours studying.\n\nNote: The point where $y=3.75$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549977 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/31/31932ff71e679d32ba3abee0276f7a039e5f204649e63feffeb13029eabc5b88.png)\n\nAn ice cream shop wants to determine the relationship between the outside temperature $x$ (in degrees Celsius) and the number of customers $y$ they serve. The scatter plot above shows the information they collected over several days. The equation of the trend line is $y=2.25x-19.$\n\nEstimate the outside temperature if they served $53$ customers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8bba2235-837e-5ee6-9f31-af7dd59da4b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549977 #edb/ref 1074 "To predict the outside temperature knowing that they served $53$ customers, we need to substitute $y=53$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.25x-19 \\\\ 53 & =2.25x-19 \\\\ 2.25x & =72 \\\\ x & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, the outside temperature is approximately ${32}^{∘}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\nNote: The point where $y=53$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549994 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549994 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549994 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/21/2130538a141293fda6b1bf07c1f0e8e5f7c347a2c688074689b1c13583a11bd2.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $y$ when $x=20.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,23)$ and $(9,27).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf3f9846-0d01-5263-932f-08763c5ce37a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549994 #edb/ref 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/a0/a00942c18663c8e9ea2dd2c5354bbe177f7f8b35a4568c5234b9c92b844f196a.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,23)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(9,27).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27-23}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{8} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,23):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 23 & =0.5(1)+b \\\\ 23 & =0.5+b \\\\ b & =22.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.5x+22.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=20$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+22.5 \\\\  & =0.5(20)+22.5 \\\\  & =10+22.5 \\\\  & =32.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=20$ we have $y≈32.5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=20$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550011 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9a/9aa9f94a8de990bdb8332f2efd5c8e945c9bec4847855eebc3fe7f6a3a02a175.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points. Estimate the value of $y$ when $x=23.$ \n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear, and that the trend line passes through the points $(11,8)$ and $(15,7).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0d9aadf-c429-54b7-bed3-89ab52703eb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550011 #edb/ref 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/b9/b9dbed9bab9d769f7408006854bd0c159cb980bfa5f4441fe1b4e5753dfbc078.png)\n\nOur trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(11,8)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(15,7).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-8}{15-11} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =-0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.25$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.25x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(11,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.25x+b \\\\ 8 & =-0.25(11)+b \\\\ 8 & =-2.75+b \\\\ b & =10.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.25x+10.75.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=23$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.25x+10.75 \\\\  & =-0.25(23)+10.75 \\\\  & =-5.75+10.75 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=23$ we have $y≈5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=23$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550016 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550016 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6e/6eb063fffcfd0464cbd8cf79811d9ed2ae1e820a30ec621f50e0cc3b9e150e35.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above gives the correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$ for some data points.\nThe equation of the trend line is $y=5-0.25x.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the value of $y$ when $x=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f611e328-34e9-5ebd-abcf-dae6da9bf9cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550016 #edb/ref 1074 "We can estimate the required value by substituting $x=10$ into the equation of the trend line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5-0.25x \\\\  & =5-0.25(10) \\\\  & =5-2.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, when $x=10,$ we have $y≈2.5.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=10$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
